A||||6||fierospeeder||oil pan||||1
Z||000000||fierospeeder||11-27-1999||03:35 AM||||Yesterday i took everything apart on my 2.5 fiero, 84.  I'm trying to solve the oil pan leak.  I bought some of this rtv sealent but its an orange color, its suppose to be able to withstand extreme temps and remain flexible.  Any suggestions on doing this, i know its hard putting the pan back in, and did anyone take the exhaust out or played around stuck the pan in held it with one hand and put in the screws?  My motor mount dissolved from the oil that leaked on it, and the studs came off.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Sage||11-27-1999||09:08 AM||||If you are talking about your front mount, sounds like your timing cover could be the culprit. While you have the pan off, make sure the front lip of the timing cover is not all bent out of shape.  I used the ultra black rtv on our 84 2.5 and it hasn't leaked a drop in 10,000 mi. (put some extra in the corners of the timing cover, pan mating surface) and yes you have to hold it up there and start a couple bolts at the same time. Try to get outside four corners or at least the two centers on both sides. Then after you get them all started tighen evenly around whole pan (generally criss-cross)  Not that big a deal.  You are replacing your front mount right?  Another point concerning oil pans is the bolt hole flange.  I allways staighten it back out.  The holes genearaly get "pulled" or stretched if the pan has been off more than a couple times or if it has been overtightened.  You can do this by finding something to put under the flange ( like the edge of a vise or peice of steel or even wood,just something to pound against that will let you make the holes "flat" again. You know that oil pans don't get much torque on the bolts right?  Look it up in your book, but it usually is around 10-15 lbs.  You don't want to smash the gasket out from under the bolts.  It has to seal not smash.  The general belief seems to be tighter is better, but not where pans are concerned!  Hope this helps a little.  Take your time and I'm sure you'll do fine!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierospeeder||11-27-1999||01:31 PM||||thanks||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierospeeder||11-27-1999||07:38 PM||||i havn't really done anything today, flushed my dads radiator and my friend came over giving me ideas.  But i think i need to take the exhaust pipe out, the service manual suggests this, that one piece is in the way, the exhaust pipe has some triangle thing with a cushion.  I have the engine held by the engine lift and i got jacks under the cradle right next to the wheels.  I soaked some of the exhaust fittings with PB blaster until tomorrow.  good chance at breaking a few bolts.  I see hose clamps holding the heat exhanger to the pipe i guess i can get that out.  My oil pan doesn't have any screws that go into the timing cover, the holes in the pan is just filled with rtv, is this common?||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||11-27-1999||11:41 PM||||Every bolt hole in an oil pan should have a bolt in it. If you are missing some replace them.<P>While you have the pan off,,,,,,, (Yes I put part of this in 2 threads, I know, I'm lazy at the moment.)<P>At the very least I put in magnetic oil plugs in rebuilds, heads, etc. Better yet I put a large magnet from and old computer disk drive in the oil pan. The big ones from old 5.25 floppy drive will catch junk for years. The small "rare earth" jobs from hard drives are good too. Just leave them in there till the next time the pan comes off. They will collect huge ammounts of trash before they get "worn out". even them they will never release any of it back into the motor.<P>The best ones are from the 5.25 disk drives and are found in the motor that spins the disk. You want the kind that looks like a squashed donut. Some only have a little ring in the motor, those are no good.<P>The second best are the magnets that move the heads inside hard disk drives. you can seperate the magnet from it's metal mount buy clamping the metal and hitting the magnet on the side like you are sliding it off the metal. the "shear" strenght of the glue is usually pretty low. The magnets usually are paired, throw in both.<P>don't worry about getting them out latter. If the motor throws off so much metal it's clogging them the thing is dead.<P>Magnetic oil plugs are nice, but the small magnet doesn't pull like the big ones will.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||fierospeeder||11-28-1999||01:10 AM||||the oil pan has extra mounting holes in the front, but there is no holes on the timing cover to screw them into, they came originally with rtv filled in the two extra mounting holes.  I have an 84, so it might have been the early ones?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Sage||11-28-1999||08:10 AM||||There should be a bolt in every hole in the pan.  There should be no  extra holes.  I have  done a couple 2.5s and there were no holes in the timimg cover so I would assume that there is not supposed to be.  If you look at the design you can see that there were never supposed to be any holes in the timing cover.  I don't know if you have a different exhaust or what, but I didn't have to take any of the exhaust loose.  If you put a chain on the engine and lift it a little maybe that will give you the clearance you need, but then again, having worked on several of these cars, they each seem to have thier own methodology!  What works on one, doesn't neccessarily work on another!  Like taking an alternator out of a v6, ever done that one? That's another story!  Hang in there, we know you'll get it! If your pan has "extra" holes, you might want to think about getting a replacement pan.<p>[This message has been edited by Sage (edited 11-28-1999).]||||reg||1
